#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228844 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cd54adb4-ef2b-5f9d-b920-4ec460bcd199" :question/math-academy-id "e-21611" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\ny' + y = -10.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution $y_c(x)$ to this equation." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny'_c + y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y_c}{\\textrm{d}x} = -y_c.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{y_c} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y_c} {\\textrm{d}x} = -1\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make $y_c$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K= e^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A = \\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228845 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e0e14ce3-61de-571e-b34e-f56630cc2fe9" :question/math-academy-id "e-21612" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} +  y = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2\\alpha x+\\beta.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ $\\beta$ and $\\gamma,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta) + (\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma) = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha x^2 + (2\\alpha+ \\beta)x + (\\beta+ \\gamma) = x ^ 2 + 2x - 3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=1, \\beta=0, \\gamma=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = x ^ 2 - 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228846 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "344fb3a9-81db-5744-822c-1f989c0cc4de" :question/math-academy-id "e-21613" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y = -2x + 5.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is $y_c(x) = Ae^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y_c}{\\textrm{d} x} + 2y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x + \\beta) = -2x + 5.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x + (\\alpha +2\\beta) = -2x + 5.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +2\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-1, \\,\\beta=3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = -x + 3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny =  Ae^{-2x} - x + 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228847 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f89b7459-5b3d-5da3-a76a-7df3378cb048" :question/math-academy-id "e-21614" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' + 4y = -8x + 6, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' + 4y = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c = Ae^{-4x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' + 4y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution $y_p(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\ny_p(x)=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha + 4(\\alpha x + \\beta) = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n4\\alpha x + (\\alpha + 4\\beta) = -8x + 6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =-8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +4\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-2, \\,\\beta = 2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x)= -2x + 2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x) = y_c(x)+y_p(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-4x} -2x + 2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0) = 1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-4(0)}-2(0)+2 & =1 \\\\ A+2 & =1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny \n=  -e^{-4x} - 2x + 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301213 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55ab4aa1-0dbe-52d4-8331-29491c0009fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301215} {:answer/id #uuid "b3234d59-b988-5b25-98fc-55cbdac73e55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-4x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301216} {:answer/id #uuid "669440ea-5538-5ea8-99c9-0fb8326df3e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-4x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301217} {:answer/id #uuid "72541d41-5ad8-522a-a4ab-b75179903f0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301218} {:answer/id #uuid "0f874757-e58d-5f3a-969b-2de0aa96f4b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301219}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55ab4aa1-0dbe-52d4-8331-29491c0009fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301215} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec8a9c39-5604-5b2d-b090-9fee7d8b9984" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301214}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d9cadaa1-0348-5108-b0ed-d95da25f0147" :question/math-academy-id "q-331920" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=4{x}^{2}-4x+1.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-4x+1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\beta -2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-4x+1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha -2\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta -2\\gamma =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =-\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{x}-2{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b3035f1a-da0f-5e21-ac30-8bdb476d3fc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-2x}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238169}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3035f1a-da0f-5e21-ac30-8bdb476d3fc9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-2x}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238169} :answer-field/id #uuid "f49a36a3-cb09-5861-898a-864f8fc3875b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ff46deb-925a-5c83-8b7c-6a6d4330dad2" :question/math-academy-id "q-332270" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4x+2.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x+\\beta )=4x+2\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x+(\\alpha +2\\beta )=4x+2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +2\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+2x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0043aad6-ee31-5a16-9d66-b3960a628823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4x}-2x+\\frac12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238159} {:answer/id #uuid "e51308c7-7de6-5d87-917d-110f1f0ce9ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4x}-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376944}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0043aad6-ee31-5a16-9d66-b3960a628823" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{4x}-2x+\\frac12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186238159} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e36528d-c8e9-5c09-93c1-01138d1f9bb7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74114aaa-9c9a-5345-9848-19b9bf930542" :question/math-academy-id "q-332272" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=8x-4,\\,y(0)=\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=8x-4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/requires-calculator false :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -4(\\alpha x+\\beta )=8x-4\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha x+(\\alpha -4\\beta )=8x-4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -4\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{3}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{4(0)}-2(0)+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A+\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{4x}-2x+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa703ac5-040e-530e-8fc6-2d203511a822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa703ac5-040e-530e-8fc6-2d203511a822" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301222} :answer-field/id #uuid "38b7a90c-bb3e-54d0-ba39-8414e6256c52" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b28d5fe7-c6cd-5018-9178-d5a601b91e1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-332289" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=15.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\alpha =15\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "805fa3fb-59af-5323-aae2-cfa62895ebf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301225} {:answer/id #uuid "a02a2559-6128-5c31-8b49-279e748d4e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a02a2559-6128-5c31-8b49-279e748d4e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301226} :answer-field/id #uuid "37346fd5-4175-5566-9ae4-0e53ec30c5df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e254e74-aca3-5cd2-a47c-6d3276e604d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-332295" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=-20.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of our inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}(x)=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-5\\alpha =-20\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e7cf25-dbbe-5487-a493-9e7445060fbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376947} {:answer/id #uuid "e3b96e4e-7fa1-5f99-807a-a658874ee80f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3b96e4e-7fa1-5f99-807a-a658874ee80f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376948} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ec864c8-9080-546f-9ff9-8532c22a57a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c13ccfab-5d20-52e4-a53a-32d4c8c68460" :question/math-academy-id "q-332305" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y=-4x-7.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -4(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-4x-7\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-4\\alpha x+(\\alpha -4\\beta )=-4x-7.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4\\alpha =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -4\\beta =-7\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x+2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e1a571e7-3b74-5ab5-9e2c-0883e761ede7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-2x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376951} {:answer/id #uuid "799dfbc0-6a59-5101-b879-6c04e18769f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376952} {:answer/id #uuid "257b8da1-ffdf-555e-b5a2-1057d77055ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376953} {:answer/id #uuid "2104400c-db5a-5566-bb1c-c92b5fcd4b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376954} {:answer/id #uuid "b69b0cab-6362-5ce6-b6f7-31feaafd6c03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2104400c-db5a-5566-bb1c-c92b5fcd4b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376954} :answer-field/id #uuid "1532b375-6425-5c93-b0e3-ee7b369f92a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "776d5ca9-c850-5a1e-a975-705e11432c48" :question/math-academy-id "q-332323" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}-2x-2.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x-2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =0,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf16afad-30ed-5055-a86c-28affd3d8930" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Particular Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea06e80a-8d93-58cc-ba4f-64e85b08492d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376958} {:answer/id #uuid "85db2297-1a5f-5d99-9781-6143224694a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376959} {:answer/id #uuid "081d6a19-0fb7-5c09-9804-eceae088cdf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376960} {:answer/id #uuid "4fdd41e7-1716-5080-880c-8d77427fb67b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376961} {:answer/id #uuid "10a365a2-a606-5373-89a8-8d9d4b2c0b4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea06e80a-8d93-58cc-ba4f-64e85b08492d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376958} :answer-field/id #uuid "c82706d3-e7dd-5e98-b527-d5533e439c95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2af28a4b-d570-53bf-8474-03cfab3d34eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-332325" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y={x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )={x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha +2\\beta )x+(2\\beta +2\\gamma )={x}^{2}-2.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha +2\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta +2\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =-1,\\gamma =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a145f527-5c8e-5e9c-8126-676a9617c31d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{3x}+3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301240} {:answer/id #uuid "b4fb5d74-8054-59d8-a0aa-af0380bf1c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{3x}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301241}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4fb5d74-8054-59d8-a0aa-af0380bf1c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{3x}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301241} :answer-field/id #uuid "44504acb-40f3-5cc4-ad0e-151cd18d2e33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc235b3a-1731-5566-a624-628471650abf" :question/math-academy-id "q-332376" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=9x-3.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha -3(\\alpha x+\\beta )=9x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-3\\alpha x+(\\alpha -3\\beta )=9x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha =9\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha -3\\beta =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186349024 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a41db1db-77de-5d53-a5a9-4f3699888aa0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-5x}+x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349026} {:answer/id #uuid "565f4ebb-393e-543c-a2e5-4d7204aa5746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-5x}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "565f4ebb-393e-543c-a2e5-4d7204aa5746" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-5x}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349027} :answer-field/id #uuid "55924886-94d9-5faa-aed2-a5e64cb8dd9b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f3493ad-3f34-5e86-8757-be73d8ebaba4" :question/math-academy-id "q-332539" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=5x-4.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-5x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +5(\\alpha x+\\beta )=5x-4\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n5\\alpha x+(\\alpha +5\\beta )=5x-4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +5\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x-1.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+x-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f120ae90-3df2-56d4-8a21-38c801f60d72" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE With Polynomial Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0db1856-65f8-5ee2-a470-df2611fe28cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-3x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376965} {:answer/id #uuid "54d4340c-4a94-5c86-9107-747b9d65dfca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-3x}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376966}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0db1856-65f8-5ee2-a470-df2611fe28cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Ae^{-3x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376965} :answer-field/id #uuid "990c8afb-7e39-535b-a607-326439100b8e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98b14171-a190-5722-ab9c-1b6df5d59394" :question/math-academy-id "q-332546" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=-9.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+3\\beta =-9\n$$\n\nSolving this equation gives $\\beta =-3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2af80dc5-e39b-5b4e-b9ac-64f543786310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-2x}+2x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376969}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2af80dc5-e39b-5b4e-b9ac-64f543786310" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{-2x}+2x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376969} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f6274f7-31a7-531a-821e-f3a1796a96d7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376968}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e09ee532-dc3c-5770-ae00-babef0c316b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-332558" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=4x+6,\\,y(0)=3.\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=4x+6\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +2(\\alpha x+\\beta )=4x+6\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha x+(\\alpha +2\\beta )=4x+6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +2\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x+2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+2x+2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=3$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-2(0)}+2(0)+2 & =3 \\\\ A+2 & =3 \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-2x}+2x+2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376970 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bde16033-6d8e-56b7-9177-9544dce2a482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376972} {:answer/id #uuid "494a2cad-3f27-5942-81b8-1a52d437480c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376973} {:answer/id #uuid "31e3904a-5f7e-5ee1-a988-b95ef10c18d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376974} {:answer/id #uuid "20210690-7c1f-5517-88ec-e7a794d092fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376975} {:answer/id #uuid "88fdfd9a-0542-54cd-8bd0-97f7295dd38b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde16033-6d8e-56b7-9177-9544dce2a482" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376972} :answer-field/id #uuid "e79819bf-0bc6-5d97-8d52-938c841cfb1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "28806fbf-9dcd-5e9c-ad17-59ce05f99707" :question/math-academy-id "q-332642" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+6y=-5{x}^{2}+4x-7.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-6{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-6\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-6x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-6x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-6x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d505866-b0ce-59dd-bace-80238eff0e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376979} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb0315a-d8ef-5718-a80a-0ac178eb8c72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376980} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b341c2-9972-5655-b05b-6c4eaadfd4e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376981} {:answer/id #uuid "6cf62c87-31cb-578b-adeb-682c68542e9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376982} {:answer/id #uuid "56333813-3a40-58f7-bb79-b66602804e9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376983}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9b341c2-9972-5655-b05b-6c4eaadfd4e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376981} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0cb2419-d08b-5892-9362-899f035eaa5d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376978}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f1bead44-c2c5-5957-95b7-3602579f7a1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-332689" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=-4{x}^{2}+x-6.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-3{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-3\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-3x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-3x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376984 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4a29a29-5208-55a0-a293-8d5921db6d8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376986} {:answer/id #uuid "01f2efe0-24e7-574c-af2c-2fe136202d93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376987} {:answer/id #uuid "b85afa91-a789-5a8a-883c-1b338b975502" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376988} {:answer/id #uuid "00416c2c-bf7d-5704-b261-b72b19cc7738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376989} {:answer/id #uuid "6d378f09-cedd-5d80-a6c0-356fa94dd761" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376990}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00416c2c-bf7d-5704-b261-b72b19cc7738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376989} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b58a385-f90f-5e6b-952a-3811eb38d420" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "042aaf4d-7af6-554b-9cb8-3be2aece0ea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-332695" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=9{x}^{2}-12x+2.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=3{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=3\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =3x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{3x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{3x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55d61e17-d082-5965-902b-da7949155288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376993} {:answer/id #uuid "19175f7e-cbbe-5801-a551-fc2cb2144b1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376994} {:answer/id #uuid "a7790186-798a-55f2-9df1-f054b2bfc006" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376995} {:answer/id #uuid "da0d808e-dc9d-50e8-bff4-1a6b4be299e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376996} {:answer/id #uuid "9a6cb816-72ba-5678-91a0-7b6966b4d89d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da0d808e-dc9d-50e8-bff4-1a6b4be299e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376996} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a13dc07-ef86-5938-9018-5339d4e4f54c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b7704c4e-4d81-520a-bf5d-625b60f19113" :question/math-academy-id "q-332727" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=10.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=5{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=5\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int 5\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =5x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{5x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{5x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "77ba8502-9ddb-52d0-a885-33190dc04d4f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Complementary Solution to a First-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f48d9b4f-4a1c-5d90-8a61-b8d9adec1aa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377000} {:answer/id #uuid "263fbeda-62bb-5af0-91d6-a27e1998a862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377001} {:answer/id #uuid "1252bcdb-b25d-5f52-9151-33b84ef065ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377002} {:answer/id #uuid "044f515a-bab6-5417-87b3-7d470407faca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377003} {:answer/id #uuid "2406394a-8997-54dc-b3a1-a917aa19f207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377004}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "044f515a-bab6-5417-87b3-7d470407faca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377003} :answer-field/id #uuid "650c0d7c-77e1-50be-8060-872df5db7aaa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376999}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c2f8e17b-55ad-5eab-aacc-5137772c2aac" :question/math-academy-id "q-332729" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+4y=-6.\n$$\n\nFind the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)$ to this equation, where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the complementary solution, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nNotice that this differential equation is separable since we can write it as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-4{y}_{c}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we separate the variables as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}=-4\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{{y}_{c}}\\,\\text{d}{y}_{c} & =\\int -4\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{y}_{c}| & =-4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we make ${y}_{c}$ the subject by exponentiating both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{y}_{c}| & ={e}^{-4x+C} \\\\  & ={e}^{-4x}{e}^{C} \\\\  & =K{e}^{-4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $K={e}^{C}.$ \n\nFinally, we can drop the absolute value by allowing the arbitrary constant to be either positive or negative, i.e., setting $A=\\pm K.$  Therefore, the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fce7db3-d92c-5ce9-98d7-1a029894054e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}e^{-3x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308640} {:answer/id #uuid "ece7ff4a-4703-5d30-a758-2d0eaa64f87a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}e^{-3x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308641}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fce7db3-d92c-5ce9-98d7-1a029894054e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}e^{-3x}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308640} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dda6553-3ca9-5906-bdf1-513ea7bac620" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2079c55-b54d-5179-8b5d-5fa6fd0d212f" :question/math-academy-id "q-332756" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=-6x+1,\\,y(0)=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=-6x+1\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +3(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-6x+1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\alpha x+(\\alpha +3\\beta )=-6x+1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +3\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}-2x+1.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{5}{3}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3(0)}-2(0)+1 & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ A+1 & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ A & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-3x}-2x+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9af41d08-91fd-5821-a7b9-e6bbdf8a5f02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377007} {:answer/id #uuid "2ed7fa30-2d49-5a7d-a764-a06da3f9f397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377008} {:answer/id #uuid "8020fc8c-de2d-5181-a8ed-5d50d12bd107" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377009} {:answer/id #uuid "b52a7020-6da2-5f2f-81db-5adb4b15b3d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{9}{4}{e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}+\\frac{9}{2}x-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377010} {:answer/id #uuid "3c1d8db7-bf1d-5047-a846-86b7132152b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2x}+\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ed7fa30-2d49-5a7d-a764-a06da3f9f397" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377008} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dddebda-3da1-57bb-b056-a70cd1561e15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bf750bb0-666e-5f39-a259-8e815751596f" :question/math-academy-id "q-333050" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=5{x}^{2}+4x-8,\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=5{x}^{2}+4x-8\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}+4x-8\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(\\beta -2\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}+4x-8\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =5\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta =4\\,(\\text{equating the coefficients of }x) \\\\ \\beta -2\\gamma =-8\\,(\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{5}{2},$ $\\beta =-\\frac{9}{2},$ and $\\gamma =\\frac{7}{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial condition.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{2(0)}-\\frac{5}{2}(0)^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}(0)+\\frac{7}{4} & =2 \\\\ A+\\frac{7}{4} & =2 \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2x}-\\frac{5}{2}{x}^{2}-\\frac{9}{2}x+\\frac{7}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186301293 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "34158a33-c6b6-55dc-a4a7-2b5c1c753ac6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc5497ea-33d0-5f4b-877d-4acdeac8c443" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{2x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301295} {:answer/id #uuid "00adc0be-f7a7-5401-ad04-a5424f105846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{2x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00adc0be-f7a7-5401-ad04-a5424f105846" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7e^{2x}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301296} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5ec826e-d1b6-518c-8292-d0f81ca1b2e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301294}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "693b99ff-648b-58e8-8cc1-0950d6dd7372" :question/math-academy-id "q-333057" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=4,\\,y(0)=5\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a constant, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-2\\beta =4\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\beta $ gives $\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}-2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=5$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}-2 & =5 \\\\ A-2 & =5 \\\\ A & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=7{e}^{2x}-2.\n$$"}]}
